00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.090
I was a software engineer on
the Cloud Storage team, which

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.797
is how I have become known
as the developer advocate who

00:05:06.797 --> 00:05:08.380
seems to know something
about storage,

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.360
and why you've asked me to come
here and talk about it today.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:13.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:16.730
And yeah, actually, that's the
whole point of the episode.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:19.980
We want to know
more about storage.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.730
And this is our
first episode where

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.730
are we going to cover
storage specifically.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.050
And there's so many
options, that we

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:29.020
don't want to make
it like a deep dive

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.400
on every single option.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.560
Because the podcast is half
an hour, not half a day.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.520
So we're going to have
to concentrate on, OK, I

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:39.140
have some data.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.810
I want to store it.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.060
What do I do?

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:42.810
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.690
So I'm going to try
to kind of give you

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.040
a roadmap, or a decision tree,
about, I have this stuff,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.660
and it kind of looks like this,
so what's the best fit for me?

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:53.410
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:53.825
Awesome.

00:05:53.825 --> 00:05:55.533
PAUL NEWSON: And
there's sort of a couple

00:05:55.533 --> 00:05:58.430
of nodes on that decision tree.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:05:59.970
First, let's talk
about your data.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:03.437
Which is, is it structured
or is it unstructured?

00:06:03.437 --> 00:06:05.270
If it kind of looks
like a table-- you know,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.814
it has rows and columns?

00:06:06.814 --> 00:06:08.980
Roughly speaking, you're
looking at structured data,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.630
and we have a number
of options for that.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.350
If it's just a big bag
of bytes, and you're

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:13.540
sort of handing it
to us and saying,

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.706
here's a big bag of bytes,
please store this for me,

00:06:15.706 --> 00:06:19.350
I'm going to ask for it later,
that's unstructured storage.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.540
So to a first
approximation, you can

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.100
think of the
unstructured storage

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:28.700
like images, videos, zip files.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:30.720
You know, there's probably
internal structure

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:31.500
to these objects.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.790
But as far as the storage
services are concerned,

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:34.880
it's just a bag of bytes.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.590
And we're just going
to faithfully reproduce

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.670
these bytes in the same
order you gave them to us

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.180
and hand them back to you.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:46.030
So in terms of a-- and then kind
of the other dimension, which

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:49.260
we can sort of look at
the different options

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:55.280
you have, are fully managed,
no ops, cloud native storage

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.874
services versus I want to
run my existing storage

00:06:59.874 --> 00:07:02.040
infrastructure, which I'm
running on my own servers.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.581
I just want to run
that in the cloud.

00:07:03.581 --> 00:07:06.440
So we kind of have the lift and
shift versus the cloud native

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.270
approach.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.480
So if we're talking
about cloud native,

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.760
unstructured storage,
that's kind of the simplest

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:13.800
conversation to have.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.580
If you are a developer--
which, if you're

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.140
listening this
podcast, I'm assuming

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.750
is the case-- you're most
likely going to use Google Cloud

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.250
Storage for that.

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:24.450
Because that's the one
where your application

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.530
is in control of the data.

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:30.140
There's a conversation to
be had about Google Drive

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.940
and where that fits into this.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.540
So if your application--
you're adding something

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.300
that kind of looks like a
new app alongside other apps

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.780
that are being
used, and you want

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.840
to interact with data that's
stored in spreadsheets

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.080
and Docs, or maybe
your own file format,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.670
but it kind of looks and feels
a lot like Docs or Sheets does,

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:57.102
there may be a place for
Google Drive in that scenario.

00:07:57.102 --> 00:07:59.560
But I'm going to assume, for
the rest of this conversation,

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:02.840
that Google Cloud
Storage is going to be

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.690
your unstructured option there.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:12.444
And that's in terms of the fully
managed, fully hosted, no ops.

00:08:12.444 --> 00:08:14.360
You just store the stuff,
and it's our problem

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.560
how to serve it up when
you ask for it later.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:24.460
If you're looking for more
of a lift and shift solution,

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.240
you can easily run whatever your
existing solution is on RVMs.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.750
And we have persistent disk
in a variety of flavors.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.169
That can be used to store that.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:38.440
There's a lot more ops
overhead to doing that, right?

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.287
Because you need
to be aware of, you

00:08:40.287 --> 00:08:42.929
know, how is my data
replicated across multiple VMs,

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:45.570
so I don't have a
single point of failure

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:49.530
if I'm using something
like local SSD?

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.960
There is-- if you are
using a persistent disk,

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.350
then you're not going
to necessarily lose

00:08:54.350 --> 00:09:00.090
the data if there's a
problem with a particular VM.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.870
But you may have an
interruption to being

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.430
able to serve that data
while you bring a new VM up

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.410
to attach that persistent disk.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:12.805
So that's kind of your
options for unstructured data.

00:09:12.805 --> 00:09:15.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have a
quick question about structure

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.150
versus unstructured.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.770
The way you described
it was kind of like,

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:23.910
if it has columns,
it is structured.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:27.290
And if it's just a bag of
bytes, then it's not structured.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:29.205
What about a bag of JSONs?

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.882
PAUL NEWSON: Um, that's
an interesting question.

00:09:34.882 --> 00:09:36.090
MARK MANDEL: Semi-structrued.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:40.570
PAUL NEWSON: It kind of depends
how you're going to use it

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:43.087
and how you want to be able
to look up those JSONs.

00:09:43.087 --> 00:09:43.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.760
PAUL NEWSON: So
if you-- I kind of

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.920
view JSON as an
interchange format,

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.220
in that-- for example, when
you load-- when you send

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.240
a roll of information
to BigQuery, to load it

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.120
into BigQuery, you give it
to BigQuery in JSON format.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.170
So you could arguably
say that BigQuery

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.230
is storing a whole
bunch of JSON data,

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.720
because that's how you
gave it to BigQuery.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:10.700
But really, it's not.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.200
That was just the
interchange format

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.380
that you used to hand
the data to BigQuery

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.350
so it can figure out
which pieces of data

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:20.920
fit into which columns.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:24.720
There are databases which are
kind of native JSON databases.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.210
I'm not super
familiar with them.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.120
So I'm not going
to talk about them

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:30.680
and say something incorrect.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.780
But if you are super
familiar with one of those,

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.660
and you understand how to run
it in a high availability way,

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:40.180
you know, again, you can
run those types of solutions

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.830
on compute engine VMs, and
you have all the advantages

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:47.070
of running in the cloud.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.950
But there's a lot more
ops overhead on you,

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.311
because you're the one who's
setting up that software.

00:10:51.311 --> 00:10:53.060
You're making sure
that you have the right

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.500
OS patches, you know, and
so on, and so on, and so on.

00:10:58.124 --> 00:10:59.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.810
PAUL NEWSON: So did
that actually address

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:02.200
the question you were asking?

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:03.116
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:03.116 --> 00:11:04.530
Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.750
OK, so then we've
covered a little bit

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.220
where to store it when the
data is not structured.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.660
So just, as you said, I
really liked that expression,

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.480
the bag of bytes.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.302
What if I have something
more structured?

00:11:18.302 --> 00:11:19.260
PAUL NEWSON: All right.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.968
So if you have something
more structured,

00:11:20.968 --> 00:11:25.870
we have a few-- we have
a few options for that.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:29.340
I'm going to say that,
with no other information

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.290
at my disposal, except
that you're saying,

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.840
I've got some structured
data and I'm building an app,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.660
the go-to answer is
going to Cloud Datastore.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:41.850
Cloud Datastore is a
structured, NoSQL database.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:47.850
It scales gracefully, in terms
of both performance and cost,

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:52.200
from zero bytes up to terabytes.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.420
And it has a very
flexible schema.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.510
You don't have to define
your schema up front.

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:02.010
This Cloud Datastore is
available as its own standalone

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.910
service, but it was
born as the data store

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.180
solution for App Engine.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:09.924
So it's really,
really well optimized

00:12:09.924 --> 00:12:11.340
for these serving
scenarios, where

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.580
you have relatively
small pieces of data

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.790
that you want to
look up by keys,

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:17.960
and there's structure within
that data, in that you

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:21.240
have individual properties
within each of these rows.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.690
It gives you some limited
querying functionality,

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:32.360
and it also has some support for
cross row transactions, which

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:34.600
can be very important
for certain applications.

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:36.130
So with no additional
information,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.050
I'd say go with Cloud
Datastore is probably

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.720
the answer you want.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:43.680
But the other thing I would
put up for your consideration

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.220
is if you have-- if you're
working in a context

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:49.430
where you have
very deep knowledge

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:52.620
of relational databases, and
you want a relational database

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.870
solution, because that's
what you know how to program,

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:57.320
that's what your staff
knows how to program,

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.360
or you want to take
advantage of some

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.150
of those features like
relational integrity

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.030
and a very structured
data model,

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.150
then you should also look
into Cloud SQL, which is

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:11.920
a fully hosted MySQL solution.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:16.775
The Cloud SQL solution, there
are now two generations of it.

00:13:16.775 --> 00:13:19.150
I would encourage you to look
into the second generation.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.990
It's not generally
available at the time

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:24.844
we are having this
interview, but you

00:13:24.844 --> 00:13:26.510
should go to our
website and see if it's

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:27.610
generally available now.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.040
It's certainly
available for you.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.000
It's in beta right now.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.360
You can go play with it.

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:37.776
And it is very scalable.

00:13:37.776 --> 00:13:39.150
It can handle a
lot of scenarios.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.290
For most applications,
it's probably

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:44.310
going to be
sufficiently scalable.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.566
It's only if you are
expecting a runaway success,

00:13:47.566 --> 00:13:48.940
and you're going
to need to scale

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.010
into the terabytes
of data, that you

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:56.860
may find that it doesn't scale
high enough for your needs.

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:59.870
So that's kind of the SQL
versus NoSQL node of the tree.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:03.110
If you are heavily into
relational databases,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:04.620
you love relational
databases, we

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:09.350
have a hosted service where
we handle the operations.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:11.340
And that would be Cloud SQL.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:14.250
If you are far more
comfortable with the NoSQL way

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.760
of doing things, then Cloud
Datastore would be your choice.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:19.754
So those are kind of
the two go-to solutions.

00:14:19.754 --> 00:14:21.920
But there's a third one, a
third structured solution

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.470
I should talk about,
which is Cloud Bigtable.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:29.500
Cloud Bigtable is kind of a
lower level NoSQL solution.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.290
It is also a NoSQL solution.

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:34.290
But we're talking about
really heavy machinery.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.970
The guidance we give
is that, if you're

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:41.495
storing less than a
few terabytes of data,

00:14:41.495 --> 00:14:44.270
Cloud Datastore is
probably the way to go.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:47.590
In terms of when to consider
moving to Cloud Bigtable,

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.830
we recommend sort of an
approximate 1 terabyte

00:14:50.830 --> 00:14:53.560
minimum of data
before Cloud Bigtable

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:58.530
is going to be a cost effective
way to start storing your data.

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:01.210
The other hurdle to
consider with Cloud Bigtable

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:03.830
is that you are using
a lower level API.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:09.590
Whereas with Cloud Datastore,
you have a SQL like language

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:12.880
that you can use, if that's
something you're familiar with.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.030
It also has some
higher level libraries,

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.680
which you can use to access
it, like the NDB library

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:20.200
that's available as
part of App Engine.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:24.360
With Cloud Bigtable,
you're using the HBase API.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:27.230
Which, if you're familiar
with it, is great.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:29.970
And if you already have a bunch
of existing code which uses it,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:33.580
then this is exactly what
you should be looking into.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:35.600
However, it is a
lower level API.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:41.790
It basically gives you sort
of put, get, scan semantics.

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:45.550
Not higher-- there's no
cross row transactions.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:49.900
There's no sort of query
language or anything like that.

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:54.485
It's very much a low level
put, get, scan sort of API.

00:15:54.485 --> 00:15:57.570
MARK MANDEL: Is there like
particular applications,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.190
or particular types
of data that would

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:03.180
be very-- that are
usually used in time

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:04.180
something like Bigtable?

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:05.440
Like there's a
particular use case?

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:06.819
That's the words
I'm looking for.

00:16:06.819 --> 00:16:09.110
A particular use case that
this would be pertinent for?

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:12.260
PAUL NEWSON: Well, the
other characteristic

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.880
that Bigtable has, which
is really, really nice

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.620
for some scenarios, is
extremely low latency.

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:22.660
Single digit,
millisecond latency,

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:27.751
in the 99th percentile
for reads and writes.

00:16:27.751 --> 00:16:28.750
Which is pretty amazing.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:29.730
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.896
PAUL NEWSON: The other thing
that Bigtable gives you

00:16:31.896 --> 00:16:35.240
the opportunity to
do is mix your--

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.670
what I'd call your serving
workloads with your analytics

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.230
workloads-- on one database.

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:45.460
Which is often very
difficult to do.

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:50.670
This is why people have
data warehouses, right?

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.130
You have your
operational database,

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.088
which is kind of your
serving database, the one

00:16:54.088 --> 00:16:58.260
that your mobile
app or your website

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:01.410
is hitting, and getting
the most up to date data.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:03.600
And then you sort
of replicate that

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.619
into another analytics
system, which

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:08.200
has a slightly older
view of the data.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.740
And then you run your
analytics on that.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:14.511
With Cloud Bigtable, the
latency is low enough

00:17:14.511 --> 00:17:16.010
you can use it as
your operational--

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.170
as a fantastic
operational database.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:20.430
You can also, for example,
just run a map reduce

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:27.550
or a data flow directly
against your Bigtable.

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:31.820
And if you provision your
Bigtable with enough resources,

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:34.360
it can handle both
the Analytics workload

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:36.382
and the serving
workload simultaneously.

00:17:36.382 --> 00:17:38.340
So if you have a scenario
that looks like that,

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:41.485
Bigtable is definitely
worth looking into.

00:17:41.485 --> 00:17:42.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:46.460
So we've seen structured
versus unstructured.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:49.350
So for unstructured, we
said that cloud storage

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.770
is going to be probably the
best solution, in general,

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:53.650
like the go-to solution.

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:57.440
For structured, depending on
how much you structure it-- so

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:59.830
you may go with SQL and NoSQL.

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:04.472
And for NoSQL, we said that if
you really want the real thing,

00:18:04.472 --> 00:18:05.930
and you have a huge
amount of data,

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:07.860
and you want
amazing performance,

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:10.110
then you should
consider Bigtable.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:11.720
But otherwise,
data storage should

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:15.592
be the go to solution
for most of the cases.

00:18:15.592 --> 00:18:16.300
PAUL NEWSON: Yep.

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.314
And that's all true.

00:18:18.314 --> 00:18:19.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:23.182
So what about when
you want to run

00:18:23.182 --> 00:18:24.390
something slightly different?

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:25.690
Like you're saying, yeah, OK.

00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:30.800
So if you have NoSQL, you
should go with data store.

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:33.650
What if I'm already running
MongoDB or stuff like that?

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.640
What good people
should do for those?

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:37.340
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah, again, you
can just lift and shift those

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:37.965
into the cloud.

00:18:37.965 --> 00:18:39.170
We've got these great VMs.

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:42.260
You can choose the VM
size that you want.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:43.760
You can cluster
it in whatever way

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:46.510
you want that your
favorite package, you know,

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:47.960
allows you to do.

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:52.890
You can choose to run it
on the hard drive backed

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:56.810
persistent disk, the SSD backed
persistent disk, or local SSD,

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:58.640
depending on how much
performance you want

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.080
and whether the software
package you're using

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:08.000
gives you the
resilience to failures.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.170
But again, now
you're not talking

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:13.890
about a cloud native, no
ops, fully managed solution

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:14.540
at that point.

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:16.460
Like there's a lot more
responsibility on you

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:17.940
to understand what's going on.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.165
It's just like running
it on your own machines.

00:19:20.165 --> 00:19:22.290
It just-- it happens to be
running on our machines,

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:22.882
instead.

00:19:22.882 --> 00:19:23.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I see.

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.575
MARK MANDEL: We've talked in
the past kind of like computer

00:19:25.575 --> 00:19:26.201
as a continuum.

00:19:26.201 --> 00:19:27.908
This is almost like--
this is pretty much

00:19:27.908 --> 00:19:29.230
storage as a continuum.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:29.960
PAUL NEWSON: Yeah, basically.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Do you want
us to run everything for you,

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.030
and manage it and all
that stuff for you?

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:35.520
Or do you want to just create
your own persistent disks

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.049
and worry about what the IOPS
are on this particular SSD?

00:19:38.049 --> 00:19:38.840
PAUL NEWSON: Right.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.298
So there's one other-- there's
one other structured storage

00:19:41.298 --> 00:19:45.400
solution we have, which is
definitely worth talking about,

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.460
and that's BigQuery.

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:50.400
A lot of people will
approach our platform,

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:53.020
and they'll look at
the storage selection,

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:55.384
in the storage section
in our console,

00:19:55.384 --> 00:19:56.800
and they won't see
BigQuery there,

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:58.520
because it's hiding
under Big Data.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:01.850
Where it should be, because it's
a really great analytics tool.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.170
That is its natural home.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:07.800
So a lot of people won't
think about using it

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.770
as a storage medium.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:13.750
But it's actually a fantastic
way to store your data.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:17.830
It costs you the same per
byte to store archival data

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:20.930
in BigQuery that it would
cost you to store it

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:24.610
in Cloud Storage, durable
reduced availability

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:27.170
class of storage, for example.

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:33.300
The price of those two types of
storage is the same per byte.

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.293
But by storing it
in BigQuery, you

00:20:35.293 --> 00:20:37.240
can run a SQL query against it.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:41.230
And you can run massive
SQL queries against it.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:43.260
And talking about the
continuum of computing,

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:45.854
the continuum of
storage here, you

00:20:45.854 --> 00:20:47.770
don't need to say-- you
don't need to tell us,

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:49.686
when you run a BigQuery
query, oh, by the way,

00:20:49.686 --> 00:20:53.670
I'd like you to dedicate 10 to
32 core servers to this query,

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:54.170
please.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:55.960
And I need this much throughput.

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:58.770
You just give us your query--
you give us your data.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:02.230
Then you give us your query,
and we give you the answer

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:03.800
as fast as we can.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:05.860
And we have these
very large clusters

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:07.630
that are already
provisioned, which

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:10.860
are this sort of
massive shared tenant

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.400
solution, so that-- you know,
what I'm running my query,

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:16.890
I get to chew up a huge
portion of those resources.

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:18.590
And then my query is done.

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:20.740
In the next 10 seconds,
someone else's query

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:22.010
is taking up those resources.

00:21:22.010 --> 00:21:25.920
So you get these
sub-minute slices

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:29.020
of time on these huge
analytics clusters

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.480
to answer just the
question you're asking it.

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:33.950
It's really quite
an impressive model,

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:37.080
and I encourage you
to-- particularly

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.380
for data that is not on
your serving path, data

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:40.880
that you are going
to primarily use

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:44.152
to gain insights from-- put it
in BigQuery and keep it there.

00:21:44.152 --> 00:21:46.610
And then you can always do
these ad hoc queries against it,

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.140
in the SQL language that
you and your analysts

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:52.690
probably already know and
are very proficient with.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:55.350
Now, somebody might
say, but wait,

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.092
I need to run a
dataflow against it.

00:21:57.092 --> 00:21:58.800
Because I'm doing
something like building

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.125
a machine learning model, for
example, which I can't do.

00:22:02.125 --> 00:22:04.000
You know, I've got to
run my own custom code,

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.430
and I can't do that in SQL.

00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:10.234
And there are some
features of BigQuery

00:22:10.234 --> 00:22:11.650
where you can plug
code in, but it

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.080
is far more structured than
what you could do in something

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.880
like Dataflow.

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:18.010
So in that scenario, that's OK.

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.760
You can still read all of your
information out of BigQuery

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.000
in your Dataflow job--
or your MapReduce job,

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.900
if you're using Dataproc
with our connectors.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:29.060
So you're not limited to only
using SQL for your analysis.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:32.520
So there's really no reason
to not store your long term

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.570
archival analytics
data in BigQuery.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:38.770
That's where you should be using
it for your data warehouse.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:40.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.940
OK, so I think that now I almost
understand all our options

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:47.810
that we have for Cloud Storage.

00:22:47.810 --> 00:22:51.490
So just to go over all
of them quite quick,

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:55.240
we made a distinction
between what we're saying

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.490
is pretty much managed
in Cloud Native.

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:02.000
And we still have a bunch of
other possibilities, which

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:03.670
is like you build
it on top of VMs,

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:05.590
and you install whatever
you feel like it.

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:08.590
But for the ones that we
propose as Cloud Natives,

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:10.650
we have Cloud Storage
for unstructured

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:17.840
and then Cloud SQL for
SQL, Datastore for NoSQL,

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:22.016
and Bigtable for NoSQL
that runs incredibly fast.

00:23:22.016 --> 00:23:23.140
MARK MANDEL: And it's huge.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.431
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And it's huge.

00:23:24.431 --> 00:23:27.460
And then we also have BigQuery,
which I really had not

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.720
think about a storage solution.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:31.912
But I really-- yeah, I
can definitely see it.

00:23:31.912 --> 00:23:32.620
I really like it.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:33.400
MARK MANDEL: It's
interesting, actually.

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:34.816
You could almost
say that BigQuery

00:23:34.816 --> 00:23:37.230
sits in a weird space
between like SQL and NoSQL.

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.970
Because you can do fun stuff,
like query JSON out of it.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:40.887
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:23:40.887 --> 00:23:43.136
MARK MANDEL: But still have
a schema at the same time,

00:23:43.136 --> 00:23:43.845
if you wanted to.

00:23:43.845 --> 00:23:46.135
FRANCESC CAMPOY: BigQuery's
kind of an amazing product,

00:23:46.135 --> 00:23:46.740
actually.

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:50.000
It's in the middle of
many, many, many cool tools

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:51.270
that we have at Google.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:53.842
Cool, so is there
anything else that you

00:23:53.842 --> 00:23:56.300
would like to mention before
we finish with this interview?

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.980
PAUL NEWSON: That's
a fine question.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:23:59.990
Let's see.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:02.670
Well, we've touched on
the big managed services.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.440
I guess the one thing
I'd like to leave

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:10.420
is, there is definitely a
place for lifting and shifting

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:18.160
existing applications into the
cloud as a way to get a win out

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.150
of using the public
cloud without having

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:22.330
to do a lot of redevelopment.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:25.384
So I certainly don't
want to discourage people

00:24:25.384 --> 00:24:26.050
from doing that.

00:24:26.050 --> 00:24:28.810
However, if you're
writing something new,

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:31.070
I highly, highly,
highly encourage you,

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:34.960
even if it's not what you're
currently familiar with, to,

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:37.390
instead of doing
a lift and shift

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:39.740
where you're running your
own thing on your own VMs,

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:42.200
and you're taking on all
that operational burden,

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.100
I highly encourage
you to see, can I

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:48.890
build my application on these
Cloud Native, fully managed,

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:50.520
no ops solutions?

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:56.300
Because they will allow you to
scale very, very high, very,

00:24:56.300 --> 00:25:00.470
very easily, without hiring
an equal number of humans

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:02.290
to the amount you
scale to manage

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:05.340
the operations of these things.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:09.990
So if you are approaching the
cloud and doing something new,

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.360
I highly encourage you to
preferentially investigate

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:18.864
the fully managed, no ops
solutions we have to offer.

00:25:18.864 --> 00:25:19.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:21.770
I definitely agree
with that, especially

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:24.040
not only for storage,
but also for computing,

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:25.090
as we were talking.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:28.590
If you were able to run
your code on App Engine,

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:30.950
it will pay off very, very soon.

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:34.210
Because you don't need to
manage absolutely anything.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:37.030
So yeah, that's a great
piece of advice, I think.

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:38.160
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

