Java 8 in standard App Engine.

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:51.464
It's a long discussed topic
of monumental proportions.

00:26:51.464 --> 00:26:52.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:53.921
MARK MANDEL: But
what sort of options

00:26:53.921 --> 00:26:56.797
do you have if you want to start
using Java 8 on App Engine?

00:26:56.797 --> 00:26:57.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:02.980
So the quick answer is Java 8
on App Engine doesn't work yet.

00:27:02.980 --> 00:27:03.730
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:05.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: On
classic App Engine,

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:07.440
what we call normal App Engine.

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:11.022
But, if you want to, you can
use it directly on managed VMs.

00:27:11.022 --> 00:27:11.730
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:12.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So it's something

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.510
that works really
well, and you can get

00:27:14.510 --> 00:27:15.861
all the nice things of Java 8.

00:27:15.861 --> 00:27:16.860
MARK MANDEL: Definitely.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:19.109
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Now the
thing is that we are actually

00:27:19.109 --> 00:27:22.450
working on having Java 8
supported on the standard App

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:23.250
Engine.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:26.950
But App Engine is a
pretty complex product,

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:28.460
has a different runtime.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:34.030
We changed a little-- we make
it so it's very safe, and scales

00:27:34.030 --> 00:27:34.790
very well.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:37.509
So that takes
engineering effort.

00:27:37.509 --> 00:27:39.050
MARK MANDEL: It's
not an easy process

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:39.920
by any stretch of
the imagination.

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:41.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But we're
definitely working on it.

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:43.125
We know that people want it.

00:27:43.125 --> 00:27:44.875
And, I mean, we're
developer advocates,

00:27:44.875 --> 00:27:46.415
so we're definitely
pushing also.

00:27:46.415 --> 00:27:48.040
MARK MANDEL: We
appreciate it, as well.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:49.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We're
also pushing internally

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:50.751
to make sure that this happens.

00:27:50.751 --> 00:27:52.130
And it will happen.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:52.630
When?

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:56.200
Well, we cannot really say
a date, but sometime soon.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.370
I hope very, very
soon, but who knows?

00:27:58.370 --> 00:28:01.320
But, in the meanwhile, I've also
heard that there is actually

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:04.930
some way of getting to feel
like you're using Java 8

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:07.100
without actually having it--

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:07.850
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:10.680
So, obviously, standard App
Engine, you're only limited--

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.330
or you're limited to Java 7.

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:16.010
But there's a great project
out there called Retrolambda.

00:28:16.010 --> 00:28:18.530
So while you don't get the full
features of everything that

00:28:18.530 --> 00:28:23.380
comes in Java 8, if you want
to use the lambda expressions,

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.140
but still have it
fall back to Java 7,

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:27.890
you can totally do
that within App Engine.

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:30.030
We promote this all probably
a lot more within Android,

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:31.738
because of the Java
7 requirements there.

00:28:31.738 --> 00:28:33.859
But no, you can totally
use it on App Engine.

00:28:33.859 --> 00:28:35.900
It's going to make your
syntax a whole lot nicer,

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:37.816
especially if you started
playing with lambdas

00:28:37.816 --> 00:28:38.930
a lot more in Java.

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:41.840
And it'll work down to--
it'll work down to Java 7.

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:44.630
So if you haven't played with
it within your GA standard

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:47.030
